= Automaton (computation) =

== Overview ==
A computational '''automaton''' is an abstract machine defined by states and transition rules, often used to recognize, generate, or transform strings and formal languages.

Automata provide simplified mathematical models for studying computation.

== Components ==
Depending on the model, an automaton can include:

* a set of states;
* one or more start states;
* an input alphabet;
* transition rules;
* accepting states or output rules;
* memory structures such as a stack or tape.

== Finite automata ==
Finite automata have finitely many states and no unbounded auxiliary memory.

They recognize regular languages.

Deterministic and nondeterministic finite automata have the same language-recognition power despite different transition structure.

== Stronger automata ==
Pushdown automata add a stack and recognize context-free languages.

[[Turing machine|Turing machines]] use an unbounded tape in the idealized model and characterize Turing-computable functions/languages.

== Automaton and state machine ==
[[State machine]] is a broader modeling abstraction based on states and transitions.

Automata theory focuses more specifically on mathematical machine models, language recognition, and computational power.

The terms overlap heavily but are not strict synonyms in all contexts.

== Automaton and physical machine ==
An automaton is usually an abstract mathematical object.

It can model physical or software systems, but it is not identical to the hardware that implements it.

== Related concepts ==
[[Formal language]], [[State machine]], [[Computation]], and [[Turing machine]] connect automata to recognition, transition models, and stronger computation models.

